# Tutorial 16 — Testing and Release
# How to test your game, catch bugs, and prepare for a confident release.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_16_testing_release
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: You've written the script. You've styled the UI. The art is in. Now comes the part nobody enjoys but everybody needs.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Testing. And then — if you're brave — releasing it.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to review?
> Script validation -> section_validation
> Playtesting -> section_playtesting
> Save/load testing -> section_saves
> The release checklist -> section_release
> Post-release -> section_post_release
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Script Validation ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_validation
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Before you play, let the tools catch the obvious stuff.
narrator: {b}Parser validation{/b} — the editor checks your VNS files for syntax errors. Missing [endif], broken label references, malformed commands.
narrator: {b}Asset validation{/b} — are all referenced images, audio files, and backgrounds actually present?
narrator: {b}Label graph{/b} — does every [jump], [goto], and choice arrow point to an existing label?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Run validation before every playtest session. It takes seconds and catches hours of debugging.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: A clean validation pass doesn't mean the game works — it means the script is structurally sound. Logic bugs still need human eyes.
> Next: Playtesting -> section_playtesting
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Playtesting ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_playtesting
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: There is no substitute for playing your own game. Here's how to do it systematically:
narrator: {b}Play every branch.{/b} Click every choice. Follow every path to its end.
narrator: {b}Watch the pacing.{/b} Does the text speed feel right? Are there awkward pauses? Lines that scroll too fast?
narrator: {b}Check character staging.{/b} Is anyone appearing on the wrong side? Do expressions match the dialogue?
narrator: {b}Listen to the audio.{/b} Does the music loop smoothly? Are sound effects timed correctly?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Keep a notepad open while you play. Write down every issue, no matter how small. Fix them in batches.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Then get someone else to play. Fresh eyes catch what you've gone blind to after the 50th read.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The best testers are people who've never seen your game before. Watch them play. Don't explain anything.
> Next: Save/load -> section_saves
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Save/Load Testing ──────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_saves
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Save/load is where most subtle bugs hide. Test these scenarios:
narrator: {b}Save mid-dialogue.{/b} Reload. Does the text resume from the right line?
narrator: {b}Save before a choice.{/b} Reload. Are the choices still there?
narrator: {b}Save after a branch.{/b} Reload. Are your variables correct? Does the story remember what you chose?
narrator: {b}Save in a subroutine.{/b} Reload. Does [return] still work?
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Also test edge cases:
narrator: {b}Save at the very start{/b} of a file. Reload.
narrator: {b}Save at the very end{/b} of a scene. Reload.
narrator: {b}Overwrite an existing save.{/b} Load the old save — does it still work?
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: If saving and loading works across every scene transition, you're in good shape.
> Next: Release checklist -> section_release
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── The Release Checklist ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_release
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The release checklist. Run through this before you ship:
narrator: {b}All branches tested.{/b} Every choice leads somewhere. No dead ends.
narrator: {b}All assets present.{/b} No placeholder images, no missing audio.
narrator: {b}Variables verified.{/b} Counters, flags, and conditions produce correct outcomes.
narrator: {b}Text proofread.{/b} Spelling, grammar, punctuation. Read every line.
narrator: {b}UI tested at target resolution.{/b} Nothing overlaps, nothing is cut off.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Also:
narrator: {b}Locale files complete{/b} (if localized). No raw keys visible in any language.
narrator: {b}Save/load verified{/b} at every critical point.
narrator: {b}Menu navigation works.{/b} Main menu, settings, save, load — all reachable and functional.
narrator: {b}Credits/endings all reachable.{/b} Can the player actually finish the game?
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Print this list. Check every item. If even one fails, you're not ready to ship.
> Next: Post-release -> section_post_release
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Post-Release ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_post_release
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You shipped! Congratulations. But the work isn't quite over:
narrator: {b}Monitor feedback.{/b} Players will find bugs you didn't. Respond quickly to critical ones.
narrator: {b}Keep a changelog.{/b} Document every fix, every update. Players appreciate transparency.
narrator: {b}Don't panic-patch.{/b} Test your fixes before pushing them. A bad hotfix is worse than the original bug.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And the most important post-release advice:
narrator: {b}Take a break.{/b} You earned it. Come back to feedback with fresh energy.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Every shipped game teaches you more than ten unfinished ones. Be proud of what you built.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Quiz yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Testing and release cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}Validation:{/b} Run parser and asset checks before every playtest.
narrator: {b}Playtesting:{/b} Play every branch. Watch pacing. Get fresh eyes.
narrator: {b}Saves:{/b} Test save/load at mid-dialogue, choices, branches, and scene edges.
narrator: {b}Checklist:{/b} All branches, all assets, all variables, all text, all resolutions.
narrator: {b}Release:{/b} Don't ship until every checklist item passes.
narrator: {b}Post-release:{/b} Monitor, changelog, test fixes, take breaks.
narrator: {b}Golden rule:{/b} If you haven't played it, it's broken.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's the last tutorial. All 16 lessons — from dialogue basics to release day. You're ready.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Final quiz. Let's see what you've learned across all 16 tutorials.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 1: You find a bug where a choice leads nowhere. What's the most likely cause?
> Missing @label for the choice target -> practice_q1_label
> A typo in the variable name -> practice_q1_var
> The save file is corrupted -> practice_q1_save

@label practice_q1_label
[inc practice_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's it. Every choice arrow needs a matching @label. If it's missing or misspelled, the engine can't navigate there.
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q1_var
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Variable typos cause different bugs — wrong branch conditions, not missing targets. The issue here is a missing label.
[set practice_score 0]
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q1_save
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Save corruption is rare and wouldn't cause a choice to fail. This is simpler — a missing @label.
[set practice_score 0]
[jump practice_q2]

@label practice_q2
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Question 2: When should you start testing?
> After the game is completely finished -> practice_q2_end
> After each major section is written -> practice_q2_section
> Testing is optional -> practice_q2_optional

@label practice_q2_end
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Waiting until the end means all your bugs pile up at once. Test as you go — catch problems when they're small.
[jump practice_final]

@label practice_q2_section
[inc practice_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Exactly. Test each section as you finish it. Small, frequent tests beat one massive debugging session.
[jump practice_final]

@label practice_q2_optional
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: It's really not. Untested games have untested bugs, and players find every single one.
[jump practice_final]

@label practice_final
[show lavender center talking]
[if practice_score >= 2]
Lavender: 2 out of 2. You're ready to ship. Go build something amazing.
[elif practice_score == 1]
Lavender: 1 out of 2. Close! Review the testing section if you want to sharpen up.
[else]
Lavender: 0 out of 2. That's okay — these tutorials are always here when you need a refresher.
[endif]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's the end of all 16 tutorials. Everything from your first dialogue line to release day.
Lavender: Now go make a visual novel that matters. I'll be here if you need me.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_3]
